export const ENERGY_SYMMETRY: FleetText[] = [
  { domain: "coal", text: "Control 1 — Odisha under BJD (not BJP): the same lens (mining companies → ruling party via bonds, MDO/lease dependence on the state) produces an equally dense picture: Essel Mining Rs 174.5 cr, JSPL Rs 100 cr, Rungta Sons Rs 50 cr, Vedanta Rs 40 cr, Utkal Alumina Rs 70 cr to BJD (Rs 775.5 cr from 42 donors, mostly mining/metals). Control 2 — Congress-run Chhattisgarh 2018–23: an ED case alleging a Rs 540 cr coal levy run from the CMO with a former minister and an MLA among 35 accused, untried. Control 3 — TMC-run West Bengal: a CBI/ED coal-theft case (Rs 1,352 cr) in which the ruling party's general secretary is a summonee. Control 4 — Hasdeo: the two clearances that opened Adani-operated mining (2011 MoEF; 2022 Chhattisgarh) were given by Congress ministers/governments, and the MDO contract was signed under a BJP Rajasthan government in 2008 — the chain crosses party lines at every link. Conclusion: the 'donor wins coal' lens generates alarming graphs for every party that controls coal-bearing territory; it detects proximity to the state, not a BJP-specific mechanism. What remains discriminating and party-specific after the control: (a) the DRI/FT import over-invoicing question, which concerns a private group's pricing rather than any party; (b) the Union-level blending mandate that routed ~Rs 16,700 cr of NTPC import contracts to one supplier in 2022, where the rival record (CIL chose an Indonesian supplier over Adani) cuts against a capture story." },
  { domain: "enforce", text: "Control: apply the 'investigation opened, what happened' lens to BJP-aligned business/political figures with no theory in hand. Result: Gali Janardhana Reddy (BJP MLA) was convicted and sentenced to 7 years on 6 May 2025 — but the case was registered in Dec 2009 under Congress governments and took 15 years; MEIL (Rs 584 crore to BJP) was booked by CBI on 31 Mar 2024 — but no charge sheet is traceable 30 months later; Aurobindo's director was arrested by ED (Nov 2022) — then became the prosecution's approver while his company donated Rs 30 crore to BJP; Adani faced 24 SEBI investigations, an SC-monitored process and a US indictment — the public Indian closures are no-violation orders and a Rs 1.51 crore settlement, and the US case was dismissed on DOJ's own motion. Opposition control: Hemant Soren (JMM) was arrested and jailed five months, then bailed with the HC finding reasons to believe he was not guilty and the SC dismissing ED's appeal; the Chhattisgarh CMO officer was jailed ~2.5 years before SC interim bail; Tamil Nadu's IAS collectors were summoned en masse and the SC backed ED, yet no case has been filed. The lens produces alarming pictures on both sides: on the ruling-party side the pattern is 'booked but not prosecuted'; on the opposition side it is 'jailed then bailed without trial'. Conclusion about the lens: 'was an investigation opened' does not discriminate; 'did it reach a charge sheet or verdict, and how long did custody last' does. The only verdict in this file (Janardhana Reddy) went against a BJP-aligned figure, which is a genuine counter-datum, tempered by its 2009 origin." },
  { domain: "grid", text: "Control 1 — Delhi 2002 (a Congress-governed state, verified only as to mechanics): DVB was unbundled on 1 Jul 2002 under a 2000 ordinance and one private group (BSES/Reliance) took 51% of two of the three discoms with the government holding 49%, Tata the third. Control 2 — Odisha's first round (1999, pre-BJD): CESCO's licence was revoked in Apr 2005 and the utility run by the state until Tata Power's 2019–20 win under the same 51:49 template. Applying the 'one group wins most of the privatised assets' lens to these controls produces the same shape as Odisha 2020–21 (Tata took all four discoms) and the UT round (three groups, three deals) — so beneficiary concentration at privatisation is non-discriminating across parties and decades; the discriminating variables are bid margin, criteria changes and litigation outcomes, which for Chandigarh (Rs 871 vs 606 crore, seven bids) point to a genuine auction and for DNH-DD are unknown. The lens also reproduces the bonds pattern for a non-BJP recipient: the largest power-sector bond buyer (Haldia Energy, Rs 377 crore) sits in a TMC-governed state and press reports (not opened) place most of its bonds with TMC. Conclusion: the lens is not specific to the Union government; it flags any incumbent–licensee pair." },
  { domain: "hydro", text: "Polavaram across regimes: the same lens (contract switch, single-bidder, donor record) was run on the Congress-era award (Transstroy JV, 2 Mar 2013), the TDP-era award (Navayuga, 27 Feb 2018; Bekem, Dec 2018) and the YSRCP-era award (MEIL, 8 Nov 2019). CAG's 2025 audit finds rule violations in all three eras — nomination-basis awards with revised estimates, interest-free Special Revolving Fund/Imprest to contract agencies, and the single-bidder reverse tender — and Navayuga (Rs 55 cr) as well as MEIL (Rs 584 cr) bought bonds encashed by BJP. The lens produces an equally alarming picture for each government; the discriminating finding is systemic (AP irrigation contracting repeatedly favours large Hyderabad EPC firms whichever party governs), not partisan. Teesta across regimes: the SDF government (Chamling) selected Teesta Urja in 2005 with a private lead (Athena) and raised the state stake to 60.08% in 2015 while the cost rose from Rs 5,705 cr to Rs 13,965 cr; the SKM government (Tamang) divested that stake to Greenko in 2024 after the collapse. Both regimes made large, non-competitive decisions in favour of a private partner; the BJP, an ally of SKM, is petitioning against the SKM decision, so the challenge is not a simple opposition narrative. PSP control: Maharashtra's 7 Jun 2023 MoUs went to a CPSU (NHPC, 7,350 MW) as well as to Torrent (5,700 MW); SECI's 2026 auction awarded the same private names (Greenko, Torrent, Tata). Nomination is the default channel for public and private developers alike; the method, not the beneficiary, generates the pattern." },
  { domain: "literature", text: "Control: apply the identical lens (audit → book → legal reprisal → allegation of a favoured national champion) to Reliance under the UPA (2004–14) instead of Adani under the NDA (2014–). It produces the same shape: a CAG audit (KG-D6, 2011) documenting PSC breaches; an investigative book (Gas Wars, 2014) alleging ministers were moved to suit the firm; a ₹100 crore defamation demand; an opposition (AAP/BJP at the time) narrative of \"Ambani's government\". Two asymmetries are findings about the institutions rather than the firms: (1) under UPA the damaging primary records were produced by domestic institutions (CAG 2011, CAG 2012, SC 2014), whereas under NDA the damaging primary records on Adani are foreign (Hindenburg, OCCRP/FT, SEC/DOJ) and the domestic institutions (SEBI, SC, Lokpal) have so far produced records that cut the other way; (2) the coal cancellation of 2014 covered NDA-1 allocations too, so the \"Congress scam\" narrative survives only because the volume of 2004–09 allocations was larger, not because the process differed. Applied to a third control — Tata, JSW and Vedanta, who also won ports, airports, coal and renewables under both governments — the lens generates no comparable literature, which says the literature follows the two largest promoters rather than the policy instruments." },
  { domain: "mines", text: "The lens 'official body finds illegal mining / self-dealing under this state government' was run on three iron-ore states under three parties. Karnataka (BJP, 2006-11): Lokayukta report 2011-07-27, Rs 16,085 crore loss, CM and ministers named, CM resigned. Odisha (BJD, 2000-2017): Shah Commission then CEC/Supreme Court 2017-08-02, Rs 17,576 crore notional illegal extraction, 102 of 187 leases in violation, 'inordinate delays' by the state in renewals, 100% recovery ordered. Jharkhand (JMM, 2019-24): ECI s.9A proceedings over a CM's self-allotted stone lease, opinion buried by the Governor; separate ED arrest and HC bail. The lens produced an adverse official record against every party it was pointed at, in each case authored by an independent body (Lokayukta, Supreme Court, ECI) rather than a rival party. Two conclusions: the method is not partisan, and 'illegal mining under party X' is a near-universal property of iron-ore states in the 2000s-2010s — non-discriminating by the project's own base-rate rule. The discriminating facts are the specific ones: who was named, what was recovered (Odisha: Rs 17,576 crore ordered; Karnataka: recovery outcome not verified here), and whether anyone was convicted (not established for any of the three in the opened sources). The one asymmetry worth recording: the Odisha record ends in a Supreme Court order enforcing recovery; the Karnataka and Jharkhand records end in unverified prosecutions and an unpublished ECI opinion." },
  { domain: "money", text: "Control set: opposition-run states and their incumbents, using the same lens. (1) West Bengal: Haldia Energy (RPSG, whose CESC holds the Kolkata distribution licence regulated by WBERC) gave AITC ₹281 cr and BJP ₹81 cr — a 3.5:1 incumbent premium, larger than MEIL's 3:1 for BJP over BRS. (2) Odisha: the three largest BJD donors are the state's mining lessees and metals plants — Essel Mining ₹174.5 cr, JSPL ₹100 cr, Utkal Alumina ₹70 cr — exactly the resource-rent pattern the BJP-side narrative alleges. (3) Telangana: MEIL gave the BRS ₹195 cr while the BRS government was its Kaleshwaram client, and The Hindu documented IRB Infra's ₹25 cr bonds bought 68 days after a BRS LoA and encashed nine days later; Prudent also gave BRS ₹85 cr in FY24. (4) Tamil Nadu/AP: Future Gaming (ED-probed) gave DMK ₹503–509 cr and YSRCP ₹154 cr; MEIL gave DMK ₹85–105 cr and YSRCP ₹37 cr; Shirdi Sai gave TDP ₹40 cr while TDP was in opposition. (5) Karnataka: JD(S) disclosed ₹50 cr from Megha Engineering and ₹22 cr from Embassy (sole bidder for a Bengaluru pod-taxi project weeks before donating). Result: the identical method produces an equally alarming graph for every incumbent at the level that awards contracts and leases; what differs is scale (the Centre awards most) and the post-2024 concentration (82.5% of trust money to BJP versus ~47.5% of bonds). The lens therefore detects incumbency rent-seeking generally; it does not, by itself, distinguish quid pro quo from goodwill, and no claim in this file should be read as doing so." },
  { domain: "nuclear", text: "Control: the Congress-led UPA era (2008–2014), about which this file holds no theory. Applying the same lens — 'who benefited from the law change, and did money move' — produces an equally alarming picture: the 2008 Indo-US deal survived a confidence vote (2008-07-22) amid cash-for-votes allegations (₹9 crore alleged by three BJP MPs; JPC exonerated Amar Singh and Ahmed Patel on 2008-12-15); the 2010 CLND Act was attacked as drafted for GE/Westinghouse to unlock US insurance, with government denying US pressure; and CAG Report 38 of 2017 found NPCIL 'extending undue benefits to overseas collaborating partner' Atomstroyexport under contracts administered 2001–2014 (₹463 cr liquidated damages unclaimed). Conclusion: the lens generates 'law-written-for-vendor' and 'PSU-soft-on-contractor' findings under both governments, so those patterns are structural to a state-monopoly nuclear sector negotiating with a handful of foreign vendors, not evidence of a particular government's capture. What is asymmetric and therefore informative: (a) the 2025 Act was passed in four sitting days without committee referral, whereas the 2010 Act went through a standing committee; (b) the 2025 change flips the 2010 supplier-recourse position that BJP itself had demanded in opposition; (c) the domestic-private ownership door is new. Those three are the facts worth arguing about." },
  { domain: "oilgas", text: "Control 1 — Congress-era MoPNG (Deora, Jaipal Reddy, Moily, 2006–14) with the same lens: the PSC that generated the KG-D6 windfall was signed under NDA-I (2000) but the CAG's 2011 findings (area retention, single-bid Aker contract) fault UPA-era ministry conduct, and ONGC's pilferage suit came only in May 2014, RIL's twelfth year of operatorship — so the picture of a ministry deferential to RIL is at least as alarming under Congress as under BJP, and the record of state action against RIL (2015 committee, 2016 demand, 2023 appeal, 2025 DB win, 2026 'theft' submission) is BJP-era. Control 2 — Cairn: the retrospective tax was imposed and enforced under UPA (2012, 2014) and repealed with a ₹7,900 cr refund under NDA (2021–22) after India lost a BIT award; 'favouring a foreign oil major' reads identically to 'complying with an arbitral award', and the same happened for Vodafone. Control 3 — electoral bonds: the largest oil-gas-linked donor to the opposition was Vedanta (₹125 cr to Congress) — the same group that won 7 OALP-IX blocks from the BJP government, which is evidence that big resource groups fund both sides rather than that awards follow money. Conclusion: the lens produces an equally alarming picture for the controls; what discriminates is the documented litigation record, not the donation pattern." },
  { domain: "people", text: "Control: the Congress-led UPA (2004–14), about which this file holds no theory. Applying the same lens — 'who got a resource without auction, and what did the auditor say' — produces a picture at least as alarming as the 2019–26 record: CAG's draft coal audit (2012) put undue benefit from 155 no-auction acreages at ₹10.67 lakh crore (final ₹1.86 lakh crore); JSPL (a sitting Congress MP's company) got a ~1,500 Mt coalfield in Feb 2009 that Coal India was refused; Hindalco's Talabira-II drew a CBI FIR naming K.M. Birla (closed 2014); Rungta Mines and Birla Corporation blocks were de-allocated; the Supreme Court cancelled 214 of 218 blocks in Sep 2014; and in Jul 2026 the SC accepted CBI's closure report on former PM Manmohan Singh for want of evidence. Vedanta's foundational benefit (HZL control for ₹445 crore) came under NDA-I in 2002 and its residual-stake decision under NDA-II in 2022 — the same beneficiary spans three governments. Conclusion: the 'promoter benefits under the party in power' lens is non-discriminating across regimes; what discriminates is whether the award was auctioned and whether an auditor or court later found the process defective. In the 2019–26 events here (Bihar PSA, JAL, PLI, DNH&DD) each was a multi-bidder process and no auditor finding exists yet; in the 2004–09 control the process was discretionary and the auditor and court did find it defective. The lens generates alarm in both; the record so far separates them." },
  { domain: "solarwind", text: "Control 1 — Andhra Pradesh across two rival governments. The lens ('a government signs or protects a power contract that benefits a favoured developer') fires equally on YSRCP and TDP: the Jagan government (2019) tried to renegotiate Naidu-era wind/solar PPAs and lost in the High Court (2022, ~₹10,500 cr impact), then in 2021 signed the 7 GW SECI PSA now under allegation; the Naidu government, which had petitioned against that PSA in 2021, continued it in office after the indictment and secured APERC approval for FY26 purchases. Whichever party is in opposition attacks the PPA; whichever is in office honours it. That is a finding about discom economics and contract law, not about either party's probity. Control 2 — other 2021 PSA signatories: Tamil Nadu (DMK) and Odisha (BJD) signed at the identical ₹2.49 tariff with no allegation attached, and TN went on to run its own BESS auctions (NLC/Bondada/Oriana, June 2025) — the same procurement lens produces nothing alarming there. Control 3 — a rival group: the only SECI enforcement action against a developer in the record is the three-year debarment of Anil Ambani's Reliance Power for forged bank guarantees (Nov 2024), and the only SECI head removed was removed over that episode — the enforcement record points away from, not toward, Adani. Control 4 — PLI: the lens 'PLI favours the ruling party's presumed favourite' fails outright; Reliance and an obscure Andhra transformer maker took 41% of capacity and Adani 1.5%. Net: the lens over-fires on AP politics and under-fires on Adani in PLI; the one place it produces an asymmetric picture is Khavda land (61% of the park to one group after 2023 without recorded bidding), which is therefore where attention belongs." },
  { domain: "states", text: "Applied the same 'who benefited, by what mechanism, denial, boring explanation' lens to a control with no prior theory attached: Odisha's 2019-20 discom privatisation to Tata Power, approved under the BJD government of Naveen Patnaik — a regional party allied with neither national bloc at the time — produced the same shape of finding (a large public asset transferred to a major private conglomerate at a fixed equity split, 51/49) as the BJP-state cases, undercutting a purely partisan reading. The reverse control — an opposition (INC/BRS/DMK)-run state making a comparably large nominated (non-auction) award to a major private group — could not be established within this session's budget: attempted fetches for a Karnataka (INC) JSW Energy pumped-storage MoU and a Telangana (INC, post-BRS) review of Greenko/pumped-storage deals both returned no content. Tamil Nadu's 2024 Pumped Storage Projects Policy, enacted under the outgoing DMK government, shows the same category of private-developer-facing mechanism existing in an opposition state, though this session could not identify a specific beneficiary under it. Net finding: the lens produces a genuine cross-party pattern (large state-to-private-capital transfers happen under BJP, BJD and, on the mechanism-only evidence available, DMK governments alike); it could not be extended to a like-for-like MW/₹-scale comparison in an opposition state within budget, which is recorded as a gap rather than papered over." },
];

