        "denialFound": "Yes. Wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindenburg_Research) says the group accused Hindenburg of \"a calculated attack\" on India, and Hindenburg replied that Adani \"dodged key questions\". The SEBI Adicorp order (https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/sep-2025/order_matter_adicorp.pdf) quotes Adani's 29 Jan 2023 exchange filing: \"Adicorp is not a related party, and transactions with Adicorp are not 'related party transactions' under laws of Indian or accounting standards\". The Fortune source does NOT contain the \"calculated attack on India\" quote, so the c002 citation of Fortune for that phrase is unsupported. Wikipedia is the only listed support for it.",
        "innocentReading": "A short-seller with a disclosed short position has a financial interest in the price falling. SEBI's own orders say the RPT transactions were not \"related party transactions\" under the pre-2021 definition, and that the 2021 LODR amendment applied only going forward. On that reading this is a regulatory-definition gap, not concealment, and the Supreme Court's expert committee said it was not a regulatory failure. The settlement is a standard s.15JB settlement with no finding of guilt, for Rs 1.51 cr, covering an FY13 disclosure lapse and auditor peer-review certificate lapses.",
        "reason": "The core edge holds and is correctly tiered as alleged. Hindenburg did publish on 24 Jan 2023 that the group \"has engaged in a brazen stock manipulation and accounting fraud scheme over the course of decades\" (Wikipedia verbatim), and the required denial exists as contra c002. The appended status summary is the weak part, because it chains true facts into an implication none of the sources makes.\n\n(a) The allegation headlined is \"stock manipulation via offshore shells\". The proceedings cited as closing it are RPT-classification cases: Adicorp, Milestone/Rehvar and Vakoder. The offshore-shell/FPI beneficial-ownership and price-manipulation strands have no published outcome. Fortune itself says \"the regulator's orders do not clear the ports-to-power conglomerate of other allegations raised by the short seller\".\n\n(b) \"Both public SEBI RPT strands\" is miscounted. On 18 Sep 2025 there were two separate orders, Adicorp (confirmed in the primary PDF) and Milestone/Rehvar (Fortune: \"two orders\"), plus the 22 Sep 2026 settlement. That is three published RPT outcomes, not two.\n\n(c) The settlement was not purely an RPT matter. Business Standard says it also covered audit and limited-review reports for 2015–2021 \"signed ... without a valid peer review certificate\".\n\n(d) \"The 24th SEBI probe's outcome is unpublished\" implies the other 23 have published outcomes. SEBI said 23 were \"complete\" (Business Standard, 11 Aug 2024), which means the investigations finished, not that outcomes were published. Most outcomes are not public.\n\n(e) None of the claim's three srcs supports the Rs 1.51 cr settlement or the 24th-probe status. Those facts rest on the Business Standard 2026 and 2024 URLs, which are not in srcs. I confirmed the Business Standard 22 Sep 2026 article by curl (WebFetch returned 403): \"Five Adani group companies have settled adjudication proceedings ... for a cumulative amount of ₹1.51 crore\".\n\nNone of these problems breaks the edge, but the text must be fixed before publication.",
