        "denialFound": "Adani Group was asked. In its Oct 2023 pre-emptive statement it said the SC had in Jan 2023 \"dismissed as withdrawn\" DRI's appeal, with the observation \"we appreciate the stand taken by the Government in not entering into futile litigation\". It added: \"Clearly, the issue of overvaluation in the import of coal was conclusively settled by India's highest court of law.\" Source: https://theprint.in/business/adani-group-overpaid-for-coal-imports-then-charged-indians-extra-for-power-claims-ft-report/1801571/. The repo's coal.json records that the Jan 2023 withdrawal was a different matter (Knowledge Infrastructure). Also on the record: Adani denies wrongdoing and says Indian customs assessed the shipments before release (Business Today/Reuters, 17 Nov 2023). In the source article, OCCRP asked Adani, and the spokesperson called the allegations \"false and baseless\". Against Adani's \"settled\" position, Parliament was told in Mar 2023 that the DRI investigation had not concluded (The Hindu, 13 Mar 2023).",
        "innocentReading": "The Bombay HC quashed the letters rogatory on procedural grounds (\"due process had not been followed\"). That was a due-process ruling on a sovereign request for evidence, not a finding on the merits. Contesting such requests is normal for any defendant, and in the SC backlog appeals routinely sit for years. The lack of a conclusion reflects litigation delay across a 40-importer industry probe, not proof that anyone suppressed the case. Adani's position is that the SC has already settled the matter.",
        "reason": "The source confirms the core facts. Newslaundry/OCCRP, 22 May 2024: \"the probe was prevented from progressing in 2019 after Adani won a case in Bombay's High Court that blocked the DRI from seeking details about shipments... from abroad. The DRI then appealed to India's Supreme Court, where the case has languished without progress\" and \"The next hearing is scheduled for August 6.\" That is one credible outlet relying on published documents, which meets the \"reported\" bar. The Mar 2023 parliamentary reply that the investigation was \"not concluded\" corroborates the non-conclusion. Google News checks for 2024–Sep 2026 surfaced no SC order or DRI adjudication, so \"no outcome located\" holds as an absence finding. It is ours, though, and not in the source. The claim survives, but only as a partial source match with fixes needed. It leaves out the Jan 2020 SC stay, it leaves out Adani's contra that the matter was \"conclusively settled\", and its closing line \"an investigation opened and never concluded\" goes beyond the evidence: the correct wording is \"no conclusion located\", not \"never concluded\".",
