        "denialFound": "Patel Engineering: none found. Neither source carries a company response, and neither says whether the company was asked. BSE and NSE announcement APIs returned 403 or empty this pass, and the web-search budget was used up, so an exchange clarification from April 2022 or May 2025 was not ruled out. CVPPPL/NHPC: none found. Malik: BS says he \"has consistently denied any wrongdoing in the alleged ₹2,200-cr scam, saying the probe is looking to target him instead of the real culprits\"; Tribune says he was hospitalised and could not comment. Record c058 (no response recorded) is accurate as far as it goes.",
        "innocentReading": "The 47th board meeting decided to cancel and re-tender by e-tender with reverse auction. The 48th board meeting then reversed that and let the existing tender go ahead, for example to avoid delaying a strategically prioritised Chenab project. The contract went to the bidder already in that process. On this reading the board's own later decision was carried out, not ignored. The CBI's case must then be that the 48th-meeting reversal was improperly obtained, which is a narrower allegation that has not been tested at trial.",
        "reason": "The claim quotes the source selectively in a way that changes what it means. It says the 47th-meeting re-tender decision \"was not implemented\", which reads as the board being ignored. The Tribune's full sentence is: \"the same was not implemented (according to the decision taken in the 48th board meeting)\". Business Standard says outright: \"the 48th meeting reportedly reversed the decision, ultimately awarding the contract to Patel Engineering.\" So a later board meeting reversed the decision. The benefit.how \"Award without the reverse auction the board had ordered\" and the label \"board's re-tender decision not implemented\" leave out the fact that the board withdrew that order itself. The existing killIf (\"board later validly rescinded\") is already half-met on the record: the rescission happened, and only its validity is disputed. The core edge survives: CBI registered an FIR in 2022 and filed a chargesheet in May 2025 over the Rs 2,200 cr Kiru civil-works award to Patel Engineering in 2019, confirmed by two outlets. The accused parties are named, and there is a contra, so `alleged` is the right tier once the text is fixed. As written, the edge is refuted.",
