        "denialFound": "Gujarat government response (dated, and more specific than lit:c024): Cabinet spokesman Jaynarayan Vyas \"said the report was based only on accounting figures. Certain decisions required to be taken from time to time for the larger benefit of the people did not come into the CAG's consideration\", and he expected the Public Accounts Committee to \"give a clean chit\". Source: The Hindu, 31 Mar 2012, https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/other-states/cag-slams-modi-regime-for-financial-irregularities/article3263639.ece. I found no Adani-specific response to the GSPC finding, and the article does not say whether Adani was asked. The general cronyism denial in lit:c024 rests on BBC, 2 Dec 2022 (https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-63817883), per the Wikipedia footnote. That page was unreachable here, so the quote is not verified.",
        "innocentReading": "GSPC likely signed a fixed-price, long-term supply contract with Adani Energy's city-gas business when spot LNG prices were lower. It then kept supplying at the contract price after spot prices rose, so a trading loss came from contract terms rather than a discretionary gift. That fits the CAG's broader finding that GSPC was badly run as a gas trader and lost money on several buyers. It also fits the state's reply that the audit was an accounting-only view. A CAG observation is an audit finding that the PAC had not yet considered, not a finding of corruption.",
        "reason": "The claim holds. Wikipedia is only a tertiary source, but its footnote is The Hindu (Manas Dasgupta, 31 Mar 2012), which I retrieved with curl. It says directly: \"The CAG report said the GSPC purchased natural gas from the spot market at the prevailing prices and sold it to Adani Energy at a fixed price much lower than the market price, benefiting the private company to the tune of over Rs. 70 crore.\" It also says the CAG criticised GSPC for \"extending 'undue benefits' to the Chief Minister's 'favoured few,' mainly Adani Energy and Essar Steel\". That is one credible outlet reporting on a published audit document, which meets the bar for \"reported\". It does not reach \"documented\": the CAG report itself was not opened, and I found no second independent outlet (the search budget was exhausted). The record's weaknesses are fixable. It cites Wikipedia instead of The Hindu, leaves out the amount and the exact date, and its contra is an undated general denial even though a dated Gujarat government response exists.",
